    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_7cba33845fd56b208f6334836c50d2ab91447c90d077c8d0eff45eb3e7fe65cf",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Fourier-Mellin inversion makes different parts of a generator control different magnitude regimes. For large N at fixed magnitude, the quadratic cumulant term gives a lognormal-type bulk saddle; for fixed N as the magnitude tends to zero, that saddle approximation fails and the nearest pole of the finite-N generator controls the limiting density. In the proposed zeta correction, the arithmetic multiplier is sampled at -1/2 near zero but near its value 1 at the large-N bulk saddle, pushing its visible effect away from the bulk.",
